A +966% bill spike. Here is the FinOps autopsy:

In September, my AWS bill was $10.83.
In October, it closed at $115.45.

 the cloud’s biggest billing trap: Idle Resources.

Here is what happened under the hood:
🔹 1. The $98 Zombie Instance (85.6% of the bill)

During a live session, I spun up a t2.xlarge in us-east-1 for a demo.
The hourly rate: ~$0.1856/hr.
The mistake: It ran continuously for ~532 hours (22 days) after class ended.
Total damage: $98.80 for compute that did zero real work.

🔹 2. The Hidden VPC Tax ($12.58)
Compute wasn't alone. An idle NAT Gateway and unused networking configurations quietly racked up $12.58 just by existing.

🔹 3. The Legacy Architecture Penalty
t2 is a legacy generation. Running t3.xlarge or t3a.xlarge would have instantly cut compute costs by ~10% with better baseline performance. If moved to Graviton (t4g), savings jump another ~20%.

🔹 4. Why Cost Anomaly Detection stayed silent
AWS Cost Anomaly Detection didn't trigger because the account baseline was small and within broad thresholds. An automated alert never fired.

How to prevent this in Staging and Production:

1️⃣ Auto-Kill / Scheduled Shutdowns: For training/dev environments, never rely on human memory. Use AWS Instance Scheduler or an EventBridge rule + Lambda to stop all tagged non-prod EC2s at 8 PM daily.

2️⃣ Modernize Instance Families: Audit your fleet. If you still have t2 instances running, migrating to t3/t3a or ARM-based t4g is free money and better performance.

3️⃣ Audit VPC Endpoints vs NAT Gateways: Don’t route internal AWS traffic (like S3 or DynamoDB) through a paid NAT Gateway. Use free Gateway VPC Endpoints.

4️⃣ Hard Budget Alarms: Set a strict AWS Budget at $20 with an SNS notification to email/Slack the moment forecasted spend deviates.

A $115 lesson in a lab environment is a cheap reminder: Cloud cost is an architectural decision, not an afterthought.
